.TH MARSHAL 1
.SH NAME
marshal \- compose a MIME mail message
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B marshal
[
.B -n
] [
.B -s
.I subject
] [
.B -C
.I ccrecipient
] [
.B -B
.I bccrecipient
] [
.B -t
.I type
] [
.B -a
|
.B -A
.I attachment
] [
.B -R
.I replymsg
]
.B -8
|
.I recipient-list
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Marshal
is a real, structural translation of Plan 9's own
.IR marshal (1)
.RB ( sys/src/cmd/upas/marshal/marshal.c ,
1832 lines - see BUGS for the one deliberate scoping decision and two
real translation bugs found while writing it), compiled to Dis and run
by
.IR emu ,
not a host binary. It lives under
.B p9/cmd
rather than
.BR appl/cmd ;
see
.B p9/dis/README
for how to bind it in.
.PP
.I Marshal
composes a MIME message from a set of headers - recipients (from
.I recipient-list
or, with
.BR -8 ,
from the message's own
.B To:/Cc:/Bcc:
lines), a subject, attachments, and (unless
.BR -n )
a message read from standard input - and writes the finished,
RFC822/MIME-formatted bytes to standard output. See BUGS for what this
translation deliberately does NOT do with those bytes once composed.
.PP
Local aliases (one per line in
.BR $home/mail/names ,
first word the alias name, remaining words its expansion addresses)
are expanded recursively in every recipient list, including addresses
that appear directly in
.B To:/Cc:/Bcc:
header lines read from standard input.
.TP
.B -n
Do not read a message body (or headers) from standard input at all.
.TP
.B -s
.I subject
Set the
.B Subject:
header, RFC2047-encoded automatically if it contains any non-ASCII byte.
.TP
.B -C
.I ccrecipient
.TP
.B -B
.I bccrecipient
Add a Cc:/Bcc: recipient (repeatable).
.TP
.B -t
.I type
Force the MIME content type of the
.I next
.B -a
or
.B -A
attachment, instead of guessing it from the file's extension (or, if
that fails, from
.BR /lib/mimetype ).
.TP
.B -a
.I attachment
.TP
.B -A
.I attachment
Attach a file, as a regular attachment
.RB ( -a )
or inline
.RB ( -A ).
Repeatable, mixed order.
.TP
.B -R
.I replymsg
Add an
.B In-Reply-To:
header naming the message ID recorded in
.IB replymsg /messageid,
if that file exists.
.TP
.B -8
Take recipients from the message's own
.B To:/Cc:/Bcc:
header lines instead of from
.IR recipient-list ,
which must then be empty.
.SH FILES
.TP
.B $home/mail/names
local alias file - see DESCRIPTION.
.TP
.B $home/mail/headers
a per-user default-headers file, read and prepended to every outgoing
message regardless of
.BR -n ,
exactly like the real source's own (unconditional)
.B $MAILROOT/box/<user>/headers
read - see BUGS.
.SH SOURCE
.B /p9/cmd/marshal.b
(translated from
.BR marshal.c ;
see its own header comment for the full derivation)
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR marshaltest (1)
.SH BUGS
.B THE ONE SCOPING DECISION, stated plainly, not as a capability gap
like
.IR tar (1)'s
dropped compression: this translation is marshal's
.B composition engine
only - header parsing, RFC822 address tokenizing, recursive alias
expansion, MIME multipart assembly, base64 body encoding, RFC2047
header encoding - and stops exactly where the real source hands the
finished message to a subprocess
.RB ( sendmail() ,
which then execs
.BR sendmail ,
then
.BR /bin/upas/send ,
optionally through a
.B pgp
filter, optionally also copying a local mailbox with real file
locking under
.BR -F / -S ).
None of that subprocess machinery is translated, and neither are the
flags whose only job is configuring it
.RB ( -x / -# / -d / -p / -F / -S
are simply not accepted at all - not stubbed to accept-and-no-op,
since a no-op would misrepresent what those flags do for real). This
is a genuine, deliberate difference from
.IR tar (1)'s
dropped compression, which still does its WHOLE job (archive/extract)
on an uncompressed file - a
.I marshal
that composes a message and stops is a different program from the one
in
.BR sys/src/cmd/upas/marshal/ ,
not a subset of it, because composing-then-delivering is the real
program's one integrated action. Two things make this the right call
anyway: this backlog's own standing rule that sending real mail is an
outward-facing action needing explicit human intent, never something
automated tooling should trigger as a side effect of a test suite; and
Dis having no fork/exec at all (this backlog's own
.IR tar (1)
precedent), so there is no faithful way to run
.B /bin/upas/send
as a real subprocess regardless. The real destination for these bytes
in this tree today is
.B sendmail.b
.RB ( appl/cmd/sendmail.b ,
real SMTP via
.BR dial.m ) -
loading it as an in-process Command module and wiring a constructed
pipe to it is a genuine follow-on design decision, deliberately left
undone here, not silently absorbed.
.PP
A real, previously-total gap, found by re-reading the source's own
control flow rather than by any test failing (no earlier test
happened to exercise this file at all): the real source reads a
per-user default-headers file
.RB ( $MAILROOT/box/<user>/headers )
.B UNCONDITIONALLY,
regardless of
.BR -n ,
right after whatever stdin-derived headers were (or weren't) read -
and its own header lines feed into the SAME "already seen" bitmask
that suppresses the auto-generated Date:/From:/To:/Cc:/Subject:
lines. An earlier draft of this translation omitted this file
entirely. Fixed by adding the
.B $home/mail/headers
read (this tree's own stand-in for
.BR $MAILROOT ,
the same adaptation
.B $home/mail/names
already makes for the alias file) in the same unconditional place the
real source has it, confirmed with
.IR marshaltest (1)'s
own
.I t26
and a negative control (disabling the read reproduces exactly those
three failures).
.PP
A real TRANSLATION bug, caught only by testing, not by re-reading:
.BR body() 's
first draft emitted the message's own leading newline-or-first-byte
immediately, then the
.BR Content-Type / Content-Transfer-Encoding
header pair, then the rest of the body - splicing that header into
the middle of the body's own first line. The real source instead
buffers the lead byte(s) together with its own read-ahead high-bit
scan and only ever
.B Bwrite() s
that buffer
.B after
first printing the Content-Type header, if any - i.e. the header
always comes before ANY body bytes reach the wire, never interleaved
with them. Fixed to match that ordering, confirmed with
.IR marshaltest (1)'s
own
.IR t18 / t20
and a negative control (reverting to the interleaved order reproduces
the exact splice).
.PP
A second real TRANSLATION bug, also caught only by testing: attachments
built from
.BR -a / -A
flags were silently discarded, because
.BR readheaders() 's
own return value for its attachment list was assigned directly over
whatever list the flag parser had already built, rather than being
appended onto it (the real source passes a pointer already advanced to
the flag-built list's own tail, so
.B readheaders()
naturally continues appending there rather than starting fresh).
Fixed by threading the existing attachment list INTO
.BR readheaders() ,
confirmed with
.IR marshaltest (1)'s
own
.IR t20 / t21
and a negative control.
.PP
.BR mkattach() 's
own fallback to exec'ing
.B /bin/file -m
to sniff an attachment's MIME type (when neither an explicit
.BR -t ,
the built-in extension table, nor
.B /lib/mimetype
matches) has no fork/exec available in Dis - this translation falls
through to the exact SAME "safest default" the C itself already falls
back to when that pipe/fork fails:
.BR application/octet-stream .
.PP
The real source's own
.B "if(headersrv == Nomessage && first == nil) exits(0);"
shortcut - a genuinely empty invocation (no stdin headers/body at
all, no attachments either) quietly succeeds without ever invoking
.B sendmail
- is not reproduced: without a delivery step to skip, "success" here
just means composing whatever minimal message results (an empty
body, still-valid headers), not a silent early exit.
